{"complete":true,"city":"Xi’an","template_id":"global-first-24-hours","title":"Your First 24 Hours in Xi’an, Dealt as Cards","description":"Ten stops that read Xi’an through palace foundations, defensive walls, carved texts, religious architecture, Shaanxi cooking, and an illuminated contemporary promenade.","icon":"schedule","cards":[{"entity_name":"Daming Palace National Heritage Park","title":"Daming Palace National Heritage Park","subtitle":"Morning scale-setter","notes":"Begin among the remains of the Tang imperial complex. The palace occupied 3.2 square kilometers and entered UNESCO’s Silk Roads World Heritage listing in 2014.","short_summary":"Start with the footprint of Tang-era Chang’an before meeting the denser city inside the walls.","source_url":"https://en.shaanxi.gov.cn/tourism/aic/xa_2120/201705/t20170516_1595189_wap.html","source_title":"Daming Palace National Heritage Park","latitude":null,"longitude":null,"place_id":null,"maps_url":null},{"entity_name":"Xi’an City Wall","title":"Xi’an City Wall","subtitle":"Street-grid orientation","notes":"Use the wall to understand the old center as an enclosed rectangle. The surviving circuit measures 13.7 kilometers and incorporates defensive gates, towers, ramparts, and a moat.","short_summary":"See the city’s historic frame before entering its streets.","source_url":"https://en.shaanxi.gov.cn/tourism/aic/xa_2120/201712/t20171210_1595308.html","source_title":"Xi’an City Wall","latitude":null,"longitude":null,"place_id":null,"maps_url":null},{"entity_name":"Shuyuanmen Cultural Street","title":"Shuyuanmen Cultural Street","subtitle":"Craft-and-calligraphy prelude","notes":"Walk the cultural street before seeing the museum collection it anticipates. Government tourism material documents shops and stalls associated with calligraphy, painting, paper-cut figures, face painting, xun instruments, and other crafts.","short_summary":"Let working materials and shopfronts introduce Xi’an’s written and folk-art traditions.","source_url":"https://en.shaanxi.gov.cn/tourism/aic/xa_2120/201712/t20171219_1595434_wap.html","source_title":"Shuyuanmen Cultural Street","latitude":null,"longitude":null,"place_id":null,"maps_url":null},{"entity_name":"Xi’an Beilin Museum","title":"Xi’an Beilin Museum","subtitle":"Close-reading stop","notes":"Slow down for texts made architectural. Beilin was established in 1087 to preserve important stone classics and calligraphy; its collection includes thousands of steles and epitaphs spanning roughly two millennia.","short_summary":"Read Xi’an through durable records carved into stone.","source_url":"https://en.shaanxi.gov.cn/as/hac/ch/201704/t20170427_1595053_wap.html","source_title":"The Treasure House of Oriental Culture—Xi’an Beilin","latitude":null,"longitude":null,"place_id":null,"maps_url":null},{"entity_name":"Xi’an Fanzhuang, East Street Branch","title":"Xi’an Fanzhuang, East Street Branch","subtitle":"Shaanxi lunch table","notes":"Make lunch an introduction to named regional dishes rather than a generic food stop. The government dining page identifies this branch at 298 East Street and lists gourd-shaped chicken, crispy fried noodles, cold rice noodles, and fried dumplings among its specialties.","short_summary":"Pause for a table that places several Shaanxi preparations side by side.","source_url":"https://en.shaanxi.gov.cn/tourism/tt/din/201712/t20171213_1594974.html","source_title":"Xi’an Dining -- Local Restaurants","latitude":null,"longitude":null,"place_id":null,"maps_url":null},{"entity_name":"Xi’an Museum","title":"Xi’an Museum","subtitle":"Afternoon object lesson","notes":"Shift from monumental history to excavated objects. Displays include Qin clay seals and more than 200 official seals unearthed around the Bell and Drum Tower square, while the wider complex incorporates Jianfu Temple and the Small Wild Goose Pagoda.","short_summary":"Connect artifacts from the ground with places encountered elsewhere in the city.","source_url":"https://en.shaanxi.gov.cn/tourism/ms/201707/t20170703_1594933.html","source_title":"Xi’an Museum","latitude":null,"longitude":null,"place_id":null,"maps_url":null},{"entity_name":"Great Mosque of Xi’an","title":"Great Mosque of Xi’an","subtitle":"Living-faith courtyard","notes":"Notice how the complex combines Chinese architectural forms with an Islamic orientation: it faces west toward Mecca, and its present buildings are largely from the Ming and Qing periods. The prayer hall is reserved from visitor access.","short_summary":"Encounter a religious complex shaped by both Islamic practice and Chinese building traditions.","source_url":"https://en.shaanxi.gov.cn/tourism/ms/201705/t20170516_1594684_wap.html","source_title":"Great Mosque","latitude":null,"longitude":null,"place_id":null,"maps_url":null},{"entity_name":"Xi’an Bell Tower","title":"Xi’an Bell Tower","subtitle":"City-center reset","notes":"Return to the organizing point of the old city. The tower stands where the four principal streets extend toward the Ming wall’s cardinal gates; it was first built in 1384 and later moved to its present position.","short_summary":"Use one building to make sense of the center’s four-way geometry.","source_url":"https://en.shaanxi.gov.cn/tourism/ms/201705/t20170516_1594605.html","source_title":"Bell Tower","latitude":null,"longitude":null,"place_id":null,"maps_url":null},{"entity_name":"Giant Wild Goose Pagoda","title":"Giant Wild Goose Pagoda","subtitle":"Silk Roads perspective","notes":"Give the evening a longer historical horizon. Completed in 652, the pagoda was built to house Buddhist sutras brought from India by Xuanzang and is included in UNESCO’s Silk Roads: Chang’an–Tianshan Corridor listing.","short_summary":"Trace how texts, belief, and architectural ideas traveled into Tang Chang’an.","source_url":"https://en.shaanxi.gov.cn/tourism/ms/201705/t20170516_1594551_wap.html","source_title":"Giant Wild Goose Pagoda","latitude":null,"longitude":null,"place_id":null,"maps_url":null},{"entity_name":"Grand Tang Mall","title":"Grand Tang Mall","subtitle":"Nighttime coda","notes":"Finish on the 2.1-kilometer pedestrian street at the foot of the pagoda. Its public realm uses Tang-themed design and contemporary cultural presentations, making the final stop less an archaeological site than a modern interpretation of Chang’an.","short_summary":"End where historic imagery becomes part of Xi’an’s present-day streetscape.","source_url":"https://en.shaanxi.gov.cn/news/sn/202602/t20260204_3610741.html","source_title":"Grand Tang Mall: Xi'an's cultural tourism benchmark","latitude":null,"longitude":null,"place_id":null,"maps_url":null}],"warnings":[]}
